<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plague of Athens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">430–426 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Plague of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was an epidemic that struck Athens in 430 BCE, the second year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, and returned in the winter of 427–426 BCE. The population, crowded inside the city walls by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>' strategy of avoiding battle on land, suffered terribly, and modern estimates put the dead at a quarter to a third of the people of the city; Pericles himself died of the disease in 429 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, who caught it and survived, left a detailed description of its symptoms, but its cause is still debated, with typhoid fever, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> among the candidates.</p></div>
